Neither is objectively "better" — they solve the same problem with different philosophies. React gives you a library plus a vast ecosystem you assemble yourself; Vue gives you a batteries-included framework with gentler conventions.

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Stack([cmp1, tbl1])
cmp1 = Comparison("React vs Vue", [r1, v1])
r1 = {name:"React", attributes:["Library — routing, state and data-fetching are your choice","JSX: logic and markup in one place, plain JavaScript","Largest ecosystem, job market and hiring pool","React Native for iOS and Android from the same skills","Steeper learning curve: hooks, memoisation, footguns"]}
v1 = {name:"Vue", attributes:["Progressive framework — official router, state and tooling","Single-file components: template, script and style in one file","Template syntax feels close to plain HTML","Approachable docs; very fast to first working app","Smaller ecosystem and fewer senior roles than React"]}
tbl1 = Table("Which to pick", ["Your situation","Better fit"], [["Biggest hiring pool and long-term safety","React"],["Small team, fast delivery, simple onboarding","Vue"],["Native mobile app plus web from one codebase","React"],["Existing jQuery or server-rendered pages to upgrade","Vue"],["You want to choose your own stack pieces","React"]])
:::

The honest answer: pick React if hiring, ecosystem breadth and mobile matter most; pick Vue if you value a coherent, low-friction framework and quick onboarding. Both render fast, both are production-proven, and switching later is mostly a rewrite of the view layer either way.
